schema: spec-driven

context: |
  # DHIS2 Android Capture App — {{CLIENT_NAME}} fork

  This repository is the {{CLIENT_NAME}} fork of `dhis2/dhis2-android-capture-app`,
  maintained by EyeSeeTea. It tracks an upstream release (Oslo-based) plus a small set
  of client-specific customizations required by the {{CLIENT_NAME}} programme.

  OpenSpec is used here to:
    - Document {{CLIENT_NAME}}-specific capabilities as durable specs (not just commit history).
    - Plan periodic upgrades from upstream as explicit `changes/` proposals.
    - Give an AI assistant enough project context to help with merges, conflict
      resolution and regression testing.

  ## Identity

    - Upstream repo:      dhis2/dhis2-android-capture-app
    - Fork owner:         EyeSeeTea (EyeSeeTea/dhis2-android-capture-app)
    - Active flavor:      {{FLAVOR}}
    - Flavor source sets: app/src/{{FLAVOR}}/, app/src/{{FLAVOR}}Debug/, app/src/{{FLAVOR}}Release/
    - Application ID:     {{APPLICATION_ID}}
    - Current version:    {{CURRENT_VERSION}} (target: {{TARGET_VERSION}})
    - Upgrade branch:     {{UPGRADE_BRANCH}}
    - Base branch:        develop-eyeseetea
    - SDK fork:           {{SDK_FORK_NAME}} ({{SDK_PATCHES_DESCRIPTION}})

  ## Repository layout (relevant)

    - app/                                  — Android app module (multi-flavor Gradle)
    - app/src/main/                         — shared code across all flavors
    - app/src/{{FLAVOR}}{,Debug,Release}/   — {{CLIENT_NAME}}-only code, resources, manifests
    - commons/, commonskmm/, form/,
      compose-table/                        — shared modules
    - dhis2-android-sdk/                    — local checkout of the SDK fork (if present)
    - eyeseetea-docs/                       — human-facing upgrade/customization docs
    - openspec/                             — machine-checkable specs + changes (this tree)

  ## Customization code placement hierarchy

  When introducing or preserving a {{CLIENT_NAME}}-specific change, pick the FIRST applicable
  option:

    1. Flavor source set (app/src/{{FLAVOR}}/...). Prefer for resources, manifests, and
       anything that can live as a {{FLAVOR}} override file.
    2. New file in shared code, marked with a header comment
       "// EyeSeeTea customization - <spec title>". Prefer when the change is a
       self-contained class, module, or DI component.
    3. Append at the end of an existing shared file, with a clear
       "// EyeSeeTea customization - <spec title>" comment. Prefer this to reduce
       conflict surface with upstream.
    4. Inline edit inside an existing shared file. Only when nothing above is
       feasible (e.g., a one-line branch inside an upstream function). Keep it
       minimal and wrap multi-line blocks with
       "// EyeSeeTea customization - <spec title> — begin/end" markers.

  Why this order matters: every line touched inside an Oslo file is a future merge
  conflict. Minimizing in-place edits is the single biggest lever we have to keep
  upgrades cheap. This rule is enforced in code review and when an AI assistant
  proposes edits.

  ## Upgrade model

  Upgrades are modeled as OpenSpec "changes" under `openspec/changes/`. Each upgrade:
    - Lists the upstream target version in its proposal.
    - Preserves all active {{CLIENT_NAME}} capabilities unchanged at the requirements level —
      only the implementation baseline moves.
    - Documents conflict resolution strategy in design.md.
    - Breaks work into ordered tasks.md mirroring the phases in
      `eyeseetea-docs/upgrade/{{CLIENT_SLUG}}/upgrade-{{TARGET_VERSION}}-strategy.md`.

  Human-facing upgrade docs live in `eyeseetea-docs/upgrade/{{CLIENT_SLUG}}/`. OpenSpec is
  the machine-checkable mirror, not a replacement.

  ## Active {{CLIENT_NAME}} capabilities (as of {{DATE}})

    | Capability             | Spec folder        | Risk       | Status |
    |------------------------|--------------------|------------|--------|
    | {{CAPABILITY_1_NAME}}  | {{SPEC_SLUG_1}}    | {{RISK_1}} | active |
    | {{CAPABILITY_2_NAME}}  | {{SPEC_SLUG_2}}    | {{RISK_2}} | active |
    <!-- one row per active customization -->

  For the technical file-level inventory see
  `eyeseetea-docs/customizations/{{CLIENT_SLUG}}/customization-files.md`. For manual
  validation flows see `eyeseetea-docs/upgrade/{{CLIENT_SLUG}}/upgrade-validation-checklist.md`.
  Functional behavior (the "what" the app must do) lives only in this OpenSpec tree —
  any prior `customization-specs.md` narrative draft has been retired after Phase 4.

  ## Out of scope for OpenSpec (here)

    - Upstream Oslo behavior that {{CLIENT_NAME}} inherits unchanged — documented upstream.
    - Build system, signing, release scripts — live in eyeseetea-docs/ and Jenkinsfile.
    - Other flavors — this OpenSpec tree is {{CLIENT_NAME}}-only.

rules:
  proposal:
    - "Always state the upstream target version explicitly in the Why or Impact section when the change is an upstream upgrade."
    - "Under Impact, call out which files live in app/src/{{FLAVOR}}/** vs shared code, so reviewers can assess conflict surface before approving."
    - "When proposing a new client customization, justify why it cannot live entirely in the flavor source set before falling back to shared-file edits."

  specs:
    - "Spec folders and file names use kebab-case and match the `openspec/specs/<capability>/spec.md` layout."
    - "Use SHALL and MUST for normative requirements. Avoid should/may."
    - "Every Requirement has at least one `#### Scenario:` with WHEN/THEN."
    - "Scenarios use exactly 4 hashtags (####). 3 hashtags or bullets fail silently."
    - "Specs describe behavior, not implementation. Do not reference specific Kotlin classes, DI components, or file paths inside requirement text. Move those to design.md or tasks.md."
    - "When documenting a client customization, include the placement hierarchy consequence in the purpose section if relevant (e.g., why this change cannot live in the flavor alone)."

  design:
    - "Every design.md for a client change references `eyeseetea-docs/upgrade/conflict-rules.md` when touching shared code that has a history of upstream conflicts."
    - "Call out SDK fork dependencies explicitly. If a capability requires an EyeSeeTea SDK patch, list the patch surface (classes / error codes) in the Decisions section."
    - "For upgrade changes, classify expected conflict areas using the four categories from conflict-rules.md: accept_ours, accept_theirs, manual_reapply_on_theirs, defer_after_build_verification."

  tasks:
    - "Upgrade tasks follow the phase order from the strategy doc: Documentation → OpenSpec → Claude tooling → Upgrade execution → Tests and cleanup → Promote tooling to baseline."
    - "Each task is small enough to complete in one focused session. Tasks that span multiple customizations must be split per customization."
    - "Tests for client customizations are prioritized by risk: high > medium > low."
    - "Every customization-preservation task has a matching manual-validation entry in `eyeseetea-docs/upgrade/{{CLIENT_SLUG}}/upgrade-validation-checklist.md`."
